From d98f0564b08019768f83d4bb4125ec0f1619def6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Pena <spena@igalia.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:28:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gthread: Don't ask for inherited scheduling on Android

On Android, g_thread_new() can abort with:

  GLib-ERROR: error 'Operation not permitted' during 'pthread_create'

It happens when the calling thread uses a real-time policy. Some
devices run an app's main thread as SCHED_FIFO | SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
for a short time while the app starts. A GThread made in that window fails.
This was seen on a Samsung Galaxy S25 running Android 17.

GLib sets PTHREAD_INHERIT_SCHED on the thread attributes. This is the
default on glibc, so it changes nothing there. Bionic (Android's C
library) treats it differently. If the attribute is set and the parent
policy has SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK, bionic calls sched_setscheduler() on
the new thread to copy the parent's real-time policy. Apps are not
allowed to set a real-time policy, so the call fails with EPERM, and on
64-bit bionic returns that error from pthread_create().

If the attribute is not set, bionic leaves the policy alone, and the
kernel gives the new thread the normal policy. This is what other
threads on Android get. So don't set the attribute on Android.
---
 glib/gthread-posix.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/glib/gthread-posix.c b/glib/gthread-posix.c
index 4646ec8..6ac0c8c 100644
--- a/glib/gthread-posix.c
+++ b/glib/gthread-posix.c
@@ -751,12 +751,15 @@ g_system_thread_new (GThreadFunc proxy,
     }
 #endif /* HAVE_PTHREAD_ATTR_SETSTACKSIZE */
 
-#ifdef HAVE_PTHREAD_ATTR_SETINHERITSCHED
+#if defined(HAVE_PTHREAD_ATTR_SETINHERITSCHED) && !defined(__ANDROID__)
     {
-      /* While this is the default, better be explicit about it */
+      /* While this is the default other than on Android, better be explicit
+       * about it. There, if the parent's policy has SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK,
+       * bionic then copies it to the new thread by hand, which might fail
+       * if the policy is real-time. */
       pthread_attr_setinheritsched (&attr, PTHREAD_INHERIT_SCHED);
     }
-#endif /* HAVE_PTHREAD_ATTR_SETINHERITSCHED */
+#endif /* HAVE_PTHREAD_ATTR_SETINHERITSCHED && !__ANDROID__ */
 
   ret = pthread_create (&thread->system_thread, &attr, (void* (*)(void*))proxy, thread);
 
